---
import Wrapper from '../layouts/wrapper.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';

import heroImage from '../assets/images/chrome/me-seated.png'
import pixelImage from '../assets/images/river.jpg';

const breadcrumb = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/', previous: true },
  { label: 'About Me' },
];

---
<Wrapper
	title="Where I Fit"
	description="Where Nick Clarkson’s experience in creative technology, technical leadership and digital strategy can add the most value."
	image={pixelImage}>

	<section class="section">
		<Hero
			backgroundImage={heroImage}
			breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
			title="Where I Fit"
		/>
	</section>
	
	<section class="section typography measure">
		
<h1>I’m most useful in the space between strategy, design and engineering</h1>

<p>
  <em> — particularly when something is complicated, changing or not quite defined yet.</em>
</p>

<p>
  I’ve spent my career moving between disciplines rather than specialising in one narrow part of the process. I understand design, I can build things, I’m comfortable talking about architecture and infrastructure, and I can translate all of that into decisions that make sense to people who don’t need to know the technical detail.
</p>

<h2>Technical leadership</h2>

<p>
  I can lead developers without needing to stop being technical. I’m comfortable getting into a codebase, reviewing an approach, debugging a problem or building a prototype when that’s the quickest way to move something forward.
</p>

<p>
  But technical leadership is also about creating the conditions for other people to do good work: setting direction, removing ambiguity, making sensible architectural decisions and knowing when to get out of the way.
</p>

<h2>Between design and engineering</h2>

<p>
  A lot of expensive problems appear in the gap between what has been designed and what can sensibly be built. This is probably where my mixed background is most useful.
</p>

<p>
  I can challenge a design because I understand the implementation, or challenge a technical constraint because I understand what the experience is trying to achieve. I’m interested in finding the best compromise rather than defending one discipline.
</p>

<h2>Digital strategy that can actually be delivered</h2>

<p>
  Strategy is much more useful when somebody understands what it will take to implement it. I can help turn broad organisational ambitions into platforms, products, processes and a realistic sequence of work.
</p>

<p>
  That often means working backwards from the outcome: understanding users and organisational needs, auditing what already exists, identifying constraints and then deciding what should be improved, replaced, connected or left alone.
</p>

<h2>When a project needs definition</h2>

<p>
  I’m particularly comfortable joining work that doesn’t yet have a neat specification. There might be a business problem, an opportunity, an ageing platform or simply a feeling that something could work better.
</p>

<p>
  I can help turn that ambiguity into something actionable: asking questions, bringing the right people together, testing assumptions and progressively reducing the number of unknowns until there’s a sensible direction.
</p>

<h2>Small teams with big problems</h2>

<p>
  I work well in small multidisciplinary teams where people need to operate beyond rigid job descriptions. I don’t need every problem to be handed to the correct department before I can engage with it.
</p>

<p>
  I’m happy switching altitude throughout a project — from discussing organisational priorities to working through an interface or investigating why something has broken in production.
</p>

<h2>Not another layer of management</h2>

<p>
  I’m not interested in creating process for the sake of appearing organised. The role I naturally fall into is closer to connecting things: people, disciplines, technology and decisions.
</p>

<p>
  Sometimes that means leading. Sometimes it means making something myself. Sometimes it means asking the awkward question that stops a team spending three months solving the wrong problem.
</p>

<p>
  Where I fit best is wherever there’s a gap between knowing what an organisation wants to achieve and knowing how to make it happen.
</p>




	</section>			
</Wrapper>
